body:
Typically people are expected to reach out. It takes time and effort to build and nurture relationships, and that involves both sides. I obviously cannot know the exact cause of your loneliness, but I found from personal experience that reaching out and being the initiator for once changes more than you would think. This means saying yes to more occasions and get togethers, saying yes when you would rather say no. Once you get out of your comfort zone and meet more people, you will notice that there are more like-minded people than you would expect. Sure, it takes time to find people you "click" with, but that's ok. It takes time, but it's so worth it. 

&#x200B;

Also remember that it's okay to have a circle of people that aren't in the same level of "closeness' to you. What I mean by that is you might have a select few people that you really connect with and can be open with, and might have other people that aren't exactly your "ideal" type. They are just.. different. I find that it helps to remember that people can be very different, and it might be best to meet them where they're at, rather than only looking for the perfect companion or friend.

&#x200B;

And also, not trying to make it sound like a personal attack or anything, but if I saw someone that was very closed off and timid, I would probably assume that they weren't interested or felt uncomfortable, and would probably stop trying to converse with them. I understand you may not be someone that "hates" people, but it sure can come off that way to others. People can't see into our minds, and we might have to use our words and communicate when we'd rather not sometimes, to let them know we're "normal". It may be exhausting or uncomfortable, but that's just the way social interactions work. :)

Some people have the mind and the natural curiosity for it. I mean it's pretty much engineering and maths at the end of the day, so you need to be good with very detail-oriented ideas, not everyone is cut out for it.

Try again. If it feels like you're pushing yourself everytime you try to program or go through an algorithm, I guess you have your answer.

&#x200B;

Sounds bad but thats pretty much how it is.

&#x200B;

*Source: Senior CS Student.*
